  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\n\nThe center of a large mid level ridge remains over the TN Valley\nthis evening. ILN/s area is in the warm and moist airmass with PWAT \nvalues around 2 inches. The primary threat tonight will continue to \nbe heavy rain. Also, model solutions suggest that a 8H low level jet \naround 30 kts will act to generate a potential MCS. This complex will\nlikely develop into west central Ohio late tonight and move thru the\narea Thursday morning. The exact placement of this feature is \nsomewhat uncertain but the most likely location is along and north of\nthe I-70 corridor. HREF LPMM suggest that the potential exists for \nswaths of 2 to 4 inches with locally up to 5 inches thru Thursday. \nThis area has received heavy rain last night. This lines up with WPC \nforecast area in slight risk of excessive rainfall today and marginal\nrisk on Thursday. Will issue a flood watch for potential flash \nflooding overnight into Thursday. \n\nThe northern portion of the mid level ridge breaks down as the center\nof the high retrogrades west across the Lower MS Valley into the \nSouthern Plains on Friday. This will allow the surface front to shift\nsouth into the Ohio Valley, lowering max temps, but increasing \nprecipitation chances across the south prior to ending. A brief \nrespite in rain will exist later Friday into early Saturday with \nadditional rain expected Saturday night and Sunday. Forecast \nconfidence becomes lower in any specifics this weekend.",
